{:edb/committed true :edb/basis-t 1113 :edb/replayed false :edb/tx-hash "57bba0e6d3c00f0e63be3e2f75425d0402ab5f53184393786e743f8e344357d0" :edb/db-before-t 1112 :edb/db-after-t 1113 :edb/tx-data [[13194139534425 50 #inst "2026-10-07T02:41:47.768Z" 13194139534425 true] [17592186092111 1020 #edb/ref 17592186287632 13194139534425 true] [17592186092111 1020 #edb/ref 17592186287644 13194139534425 true] [17592186092112 1020 #edb/ref 17592186287625 13194139534425 true] [17592186092112 1020 #edb/ref 17592186287639 13194139534425 true] [17592186092118 1020 #edb/ref 17592186287618 13194139534425 true] [17592186287618 1017 #edb/ref 17592186287619 13194139534425 true] [17592186287618 1089 "q-120893" 13194139534425 true] [17592186287618 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534425 true] [17592186287618 1100 "Given that ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are the solutions to the equation $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=\\frac{7}{8}$ for $0\\le x<2\\pi ,$ what is the value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2},$ rounded to one decimal place?" 13194139534425 true] [17592186287618 1113 #uuid "1745afc2-b971-5788-afd0-97c27ea03061" 13194139534425 true] [17592186287618 1145 "First, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(\\frac{7}{8})=0.505\\,360…≈0.505\\,4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $0.505\\,4$ lies inside the specified domain, our first solution is ${x}_{1}=0.505\\,4.$\n\nOur first solution lies in the first quadrant and has a reference angle of ${\\theta }_{R}=0.505\\,4.$ Since $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x>0,$ the second solution must lie in the fourth quadrant, and it also has a reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}=0.505\\,4.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14005103/assets/4979d3f7031e70a642e6995c3eb819d2238281c1312a3a32c2eb4fe8dd576c24.png)\n\nSince the second solution ${x}_{2}$ lies in the $4$th quadrant, we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & =2\\pi -{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & =2\\pi -0.505\\,4 \\\\  & ≈5.777\\,8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}=0.505\\,4\\cdot 5.777\\,8≈2.9\n$$\n\nrounded to one decimal place." 13194139534425 true] [17592186287619 1000 #edb/ref 17592186287620 13194139534425 true] [17592186287619 1000 #edb/ref 17592186287621 13194139534425 true] [17592186287619 1000 #edb/ref 17592186287622 13194139534425 true] [17592186287619 1000 #edb/ref 17592186287623 13194139534425 true] [17592186287619 1000 #edb/ref 17592186287624 13194139534425 true] [17592186287619 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534425 true] [17592186287619 1041 "selection" 13194139534425 true] [17592186287619 1104 #edb/ref 17592186287623 13194139534425 true] [17592186287619 1110 #uuid "33ff4300-92d1-53b6-9ddf-f92ba758026a" 13194139534425 true] [17592186287620 1034 "2.7" 13194139534425 true] [17592186287620 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534425 true] [17592186287620 1111 #uuid "4e75e05b-69b7-5d89-ab44-68e13e00bf09" 13194139534425 true] [17592186287621 1034 "3.0" 13194139534425 true] [17592186287621 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534425 true] [17592186287621 1111 #uuid "5cb4c02c-f63c-5f7c-9612-95116e8b7a28" 13194139534425 true] [17592186287622 1034 "2.8" 13194139534425 true] [17592186287622 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534425 true] [17592186287622 1111 #uuid "dcbed97b-6ddd-57c8-b99e-90a38430c8d6" 13194139534425 true] [17592186287623 1034 "2.9" 13194139534425 true] [17592186287623 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534425 true] [17592186287623 1111 #uuid "a5353d77-4708-5328-a5b2-22e0a3c0f7a2" 13194139534425 true] [17592186287624 1034 "3.1" 13194139534425 true] [17592186287624 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534425 true] [17592186287624 1111 #uuid "d8310729-e44c-5cf3-bcde-0a277495a68d" 13194139534425 true] [17592186287625 1017 #edb/ref 17592186287626 13194139534425 true] [17592186287625 1089 "q-120982" 13194139534425 true] [17592186287625 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534425 true] [17592186287625 1100 "Given that ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are the solutions of the equation $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=0.4$ for $-{180}^{∘}<x\\le {180}^{∘},$ what is the numerical value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2},$ rounded to the nearest ten?" 13194139534425 true] [17592186287625 1113 #uuid "d74b0693-7ccd-5249-bfd3-82bffd3a3cb4" 13194139534425 true] [17592186287625 1145 "First, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(0.4)=66.421{…}^{∘}≈{66.42}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${66.42}^{∘}$ lies inside the required domain, our first solution is ${x}_{1}={66.42}^{∘}.$\n\nOur first solution lies in the first quadrant and has a reference angle of \n${\\theta }_{R}={66.42}^{∘}.$\n\nSince $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x>0,$ the second solution must lie in the fourth quadrant, and it also has a reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}={66.42}^{∘}.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14005103/assets/9d2b6dbe16a9537db672ddb3b369dbb49132a9f8860c774f3b8c79d478711f95.png)\n\nA second solution $X$ lies in the $4$th quadrant, and we can find it using the reference angle, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X & ={360}^{∘}-{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & ={360}^{∘}-{66.42}^{∘} \\\\  & ={293.58}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, this value is not in the required domain. To get a second solution that's within the required domain, we simply subtract ${360}^{∘}$ from $X:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & =X-{360}^{∘} \\\\  & ={293.58}^{∘}-{360}^{∘} \\\\  & =-{66.42}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the numerical value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}$ is\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}=66.42\\cdot (-66.42)≈-4\\,410\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest ten." 13194139534425 true] [17592186287626 1000 #edb/ref 17592186287627 13194139534425 true] [17592186287626 1000 #edb/ref 17592186287628 13194139534425 true] [17592186287626 1000 #edb/ref 17592186287629 13194139534425 true] [17592186287626 1000 #edb/ref 17592186287630 13194139534425 true] [17592186287626 1000 #edb/ref 17592186287631 13194139534425 true] [17592186287626 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534425 true] [17592186287626 1041 "selection" 13194139534425 true] [17592186287626 1104 #edb/ref 17592186287627 13194139534425 true] [17592186287626 1110 #uuid "04d207a6-7757-5b40-880c-1b92a1792a7a" 13194139534425 true] [17592186287627 1034 "-4\\,410" 13194139534425 true] [17592186287627 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534425 true] [17592186287627 1111 #uuid "a62e3915-c58c-55f8-83f1-1e44a85809d5" 13194139534425 true] [17592186287628 1034 "-4\\,220" 13194139534425 true] [17592186287628 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534425 true] [17592186287628 1111 #uuid "45b82809-5996-5b21-b3e5-9bec5e7610fa" 13194139534425 true] [17592186287629 1034 "-2\\,320" 13194139534425 true] [17592186287629 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534425 true] [17592186287629 1111 #uuid "08887998-3606-56a2-891d-bfd9a1446b39" 13194139534425 true] [17592186287630 1034 "-2\\,580" 13194139534425 true] [17592186287630 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534425 true] [17592186287630 1111 #uuid "c88bde2b-87da-50ec-9443-583f1c383eb5" 13194139534425 true] [17592186287631 1034 "-3\\,440" 13194139534425 true] [17592186287631 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534425 true] [17592186287631 1111 #uuid "eea58201-2105-5006-a837-ca10eaf2abb5" 13194139534425 true] [17592186287632 1017 #edb/ref 17592186287633 13194139534425 true] [17592186287632 1089 "q-121005" 13194139534425 true] [17592186287632 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534425 true] [17592186287632 1100 "Solve the equation $\\sqrt{5}-5\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=\\sqrt{5}$ for ${0}^{∘}\\le x<{360}^{∘}.$" 13194139534425 true] [17592186287632 1113 #uuid "65cd22dc-7a3f-567a-99e3-fbc144596450" 13194139534425 true] [17592186287632 1145 "First, we rearrange the equation, isolating $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{5}-5\\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =\\sqrt{5} \\\\ -5\\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the solutions to $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=0$ correspond to the roots of $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.$ \n\nNow, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(0)={90}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${90}^{∘}$ lies inside the required domain, our first solution is ${x}_{1}={90}^{∘}.$\n\nThis solution is a quadrantal angle. To find the remaining solutions, we use our knowledge of the cosine function. We know that the cosine function has its roots at\n\n$$\nx={90}^{∘}+{180}^{∘}\\cdot n.\n$$\n\nTherefore, to find the other solution ${x}_{2}$ in the given range, we set $n=1,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & ={90}^{∘}+{180}^{∘}={270}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solutions are $x={90}^{∘},\\,{270}^{∘}.$" 13194139534425 true] [17592186287633 1000 #edb/ref 17592186287634 13194139534425 true] [17592186287633 1000 #edb/ref 17592186287635 13194139534425 true] [17592186287633 1000 #edb/ref 17592186287636 13194139534425 true] [17592186287633 1000 #edb/ref 17592186287637 13194139534425 true] [17592186287633 1000 #edb/ref 17592186287638 13194139534425 true] [17592186287633 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534425 true] [17592186287633 1041 "selection" 13194139534425 true] [17592186287633 1104 #edb/ref 17592186287637 13194139534425 true] [17592186287633 1110 #uuid "f58f761f-635d-538b-8f10-50674122b23c" 13194139534425 true] [17592186287634 1034 "x={180}^{∘}" 13194139534425 true] [17592186287634 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534425 true] [17592186287634 1111 #uuid "0019a227-6971-576a-a3f9-e0817a13c2a5" 13194139534425 true] [17592186287635 1034 "x={90}^{∘}" 13194139534425 true] [17592186287635 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534425 true] [17592186287635 1111 #uuid "2af34539-bd76-53e3-a552-6716d7a54801" 13194139534425 true] [17592186287636 1034 "x={45}^{∘},\\,{60}^{∘}" 13194139534425 true] [17592186287636 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534425 true] [17592186287636 1111 #uuid "2000c653-cef2-55b5-b9f0-f9945ea3fd5d" 13194139534425 true] [17592186287637 1034 "x={90}^{∘},\\,{270}^{∘}" 13194139534425 true] [17592186287637 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534425 true] [17592186287637 1111 #uuid "34ac7b21-1551-5fc0-ad7c-46509179f2d9" 13194139534425 true] [17592186287638 1034 "x=-{90}^{∘},\\,{90}^{∘}" 13194139534425 true] [17592186287638 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534425 true] [17592186287638 1111 #uuid "75c2803e-48a1-5ae5-b05e-b1b5b5a763d5" 13194139534425 true] [17592186287639 1017 #edb/ref 17592186287640 13194139534425 true] [17592186287639 1017 #edb/ref 17592186287642 13194139534425 true] [17592186287639 1089 "q-274587" 13194139534425 true] [17592186287639 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534425 true] [17592186287639 1100 "Arranged in **ascending order** and rounded to **the nearest integer**, the solutions of the equation $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=0.7$ for $-{180}^{∘}<x\\le {180}^{∘}$ are $x=${{field-1}}${}^{∘}$ and $x=$\n{{field-2}}${}^{∘}.$" 13194139534425 true] [17592186287639 1113 #uuid "3e300674-c74b-5fe9-8494-1fd913cb8b1f" 13194139534425 true] [17592186287639 1145 "First, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(0.7)=(45.572\\,9…)^{∘}≈{45.573}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${45.573}^{∘}$ lies inside the required domain, our first solution, rounded to the nearest integer, is $x={46}^{∘}.$\n\nOur first solution lies in the first quadrant and has a reference angle of \n${\\theta }_{R}={45.573}^{∘}.$\nSince $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x>0,$ the second solution must lie in the fourth quadrant, and it also has a reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}={45.573}^{∘}.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14005103/assets/3ff8bb94afda0843fa6f22802ae70ca98d7e6856e8be2911ab1cb3ea2cccd2c1.png)\n\nA second solution $X$ lies in the $4$th quadrant, and we can find it using the reference angle, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X & ={360}^{∘}-{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & ={360}^{∘}-{45.573}^{∘} \\\\  & ={314.427}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, this value is not in the required domain. To get a second solution that's within the required domain, we simply subtract ${360}^{∘}$ from $X:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =X-{360}^{∘} \\\\  & ={314.427}^{∘}-{360}^{∘} \\\\  & =-{45.573}^{∘} \\\\  & ≈-{46}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, arranged in ascending order, the solutions are $x={-46}^{∘}$ and $x={46}^{∘}.$" 13194139534425 true] [17592186287640 1000 #edb/ref 17592186287641 13194139534425 true] [17592186287640 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139534425 true] [17592186287640 1041 "field-1" 13194139534425 true] [17592186287640 1104 #edb/ref 17592186287641 13194139534425 true] [17592186287640 1110 #uuid "7eadacaa-8b63-5d1a-8319-eecb56549b1b" 13194139534425 true] [17592186287641 1034 "-46" 13194139534425 true] [17592186287641 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534425 true] [17592186287641 1111 #uuid "aed24c60-6961-5ff5-9a3f-7add6d0f2e29" 13194139534425 true] [17592186287642 1000 #edb/ref 17592186287643 13194139534425 true] [17592186287642 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139534425 true] [17592186287642 1041 "field-2" 13194139534425 true] [17592186287642 1104 #edb/ref 17592186287643 13194139534425 true] [17592186287642 1110 #uuid "dab69fb3-fbc8-5cf3-a29d-bbe0f31c7b07" 13194139534425 true] [17592186287643 1034 "46" 13194139534425 true] [17592186287643 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534425 true] [17592186287643 1111 #uuid "e1531d05-7142-545c-a3a8-63fc8ecb5b29" 13194139534425 true] [17592186287644 1017 #edb/ref 17592186287645 13194139534425 true] [17592186287644 1089 "q-338741" 13194139534425 true] [17592186287644 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534425 true] [17592186287644 1100 "The solution to the equation $4\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+9=5$ for ${0}^{∘}\\le x<{360}^{∘}$ is $x=${{field-1}}${}^{∘}.$" 13194139534425 true] [17592186287644 1113 #uuid "39aae2c6-791e-52d0-9e6a-2023a4352a73" 13194139534425 true] [17592186287644 1145 "First, we rearrange the equation, isolating $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+9 & =5 \\\\ 4\\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =-4 \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the solutions to $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=-1$ correspond to the values of $x$ at which $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ attains its minimum value.\n\nFirst, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(-1)={180}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${180}^{∘}$ lies inside the required domain, our first solution is ${x}_{1}={180}^{∘}.$\n\nThis solution is a quadrantal angle. To find other solutions, we use our knowledge of the cosine function. We know that the cosine function has its minimum values at\n\n$$\nx={180}^{∘}+{360}^{∘}\\cdot n,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is an integer.\n\nHowever, since the domain ${0}^{∘}\\le x<{360}^{∘}$ is one period, and we have already found one minimum value in this period, we conclude that $x={180}^{∘}$ is the only solution." 13194139534425 true] [17592186287645 1000 #edb/ref 17592186287646 13194139534425 true] [17592186287645 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139534425 true] [17592186287645 1041 "field-1" 13194139534425 true] [17592186287645 1104 #edb/ref 17592186287646 13194139534425 true] [17592186287645 1110 #uuid "4a94393f-ba4b-5b59-8b2b-c3e6e7cdbfcb" 13194139534425 true] [17592186287646 1034 "180" 13194139534425 true] [17592186287646 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534425 true] [17592186287646 1111 #uuid "efc34ef6-2573-50ca-bd34-43468d41ef43" 13194139534425 true]] :edb/tempids {"q-120893" 17592186287618 "q-120893/selection" 17592186287619 "q-120893/selection/449eabead9e7713acaede5d2afc64373661ad92a6819ce95b943eb2835d0094c" 17592186287620 "q-120893/selection/5fe81f5926450f8c072940f6ffc6b3447e362985b21ccc119966335a07ccc96e" 17592186287621 "q-120893/selection/86646dfdd34dd99b12d8b2667f99ce75f934e650a9b069751cbcf304e9fbea94" 17592186287622 "q-120893/selection/9db8bb3a8fca0a53f1a66296f215433b5fba29bca2325a9035c810088be55a44" 17592186287623 "q-120893/selection/eff549b88f0dad6